{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-162252",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,\\,{x}^{2}\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le x\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/96ac5800a3c28448548ae60889724f82961312232ea27f1cf1233788e6a1d259.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,{x}^{2}\\le z\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nx=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz={x}^{2}\\,⟹\\,x=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le x\\le \\sqrt{z}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14248203/assets/3c108855dfd47c0917ee72fc6360fb0696538165c7f960903fd8c33d38be6ed5.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}{\\int }_{0}^{x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}[{\\int }_{0}^{x}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\text{d}y]\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})[y]_{0}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}4x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})[{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}2x\\,\\text{d}x]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})[{x}^{2}]_{0}^{\\sqrt{z}}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2z\\operatorname{sin}⁡({z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡({z}^{2})]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1)-1) \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
